    "https://careers.singaporetech.edu.sg/job/Research-Fellow-Research-Assistant-JL/1147-en_GB": {
      "title": "Research Fellow / Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Fellow / Research Assistant - JL Job Details | Singapore Institute of Technology We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve the website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Reject All Cookies Accept All Cookies Skip to main content Home View All Jobs Sign In / View Profile Home View All Jobs Sign In / View Profile Home View All Jobs Sign In / View Profile Search Jobs Job Description Apply Now Research Fellow / Research Assistant - JL Posting Start Date: 09/09/2026 Schemes of Service: Research Division: Rehabilitation Employment Type: Fixed Term Research Fellow / Research Assistant Mixed-Methods Behavioural Insights Study: Second-hand Tobacco Smoke Exposure in Public Residential Estates The Project SIT, in collaboration with the Singapore University of Social Sciences (SUSS), is conducting a mixed-methods behavioural insights study of second-hand tobacco smoke (SHS) exposure in public residential estates. The study aims to (i) document smokers’ routines, spatial patterns, and behavioural archetypes; (ii) measure population-level attitudes, perceptions, and values regarding SHS exposure from neighbours; (iii) co-develop socially acceptable interventions with residents and community stakeholders; and (iv) pilot and evaluate selected interventions. The team is led by Associate Professor Walter Theseira (Principal Investigator, SUSS), with Co-Principal Investigators Emeritus Professor Ho Kong Chong (NUS) and Associate Professor Jean Liu (SIT). Methods and Tasks The study comprises four work packages: (1) qualitative research, including ten exploratory focus group discussions (FGDs) with smokers and non-smokers and 50 ethnographic “walk-with” interviews with smokers in HDB neighbourhoods; (2) large-scale door-to-door surveys — a general population survey (n ≈ 3,000) and a targeted survey of residents affected by SHS (n ≈ 1,000) — fielded by a survey firm under the research team’s direction; (3) co-solutioning engagement sessions and co-design workshops with residents, grassroots leaders, and community stakeholders; and (4) small-scale piloting and evaluation of interventions in selected neighbourhoods. Work packages (2) and (4) are subject to the grantor’s budget and approval. The Research Fellow/Research Assistant will work closely with the PI and Co-PIs to: - Coordinate and oversee fieldwork, including participant recruitment, scheduling, conduct of FGDs, walk-with interviews and engagement sessions, and management of transcription - Support research ethics (IRB) applications and compliance; conduct literature reviews and analyses of existing administrative and complaints data - Code and analyse qualitative data; support questionnaire design, sampling, and statistical analysis of survey data - Draft progress documentation, reports, and presentations for stakeholders - Coordinate across the PIs and liaise with the grantor Requirements - Research Fellow: PhD in economics, psychology, sociology, public health or policy, or a related behavioural or social science. Research Assistant: Bachelor’s or Master’s degree in a relevant discipline. - Training or experience in qualitative research (focus groups, interviews, ethnography) and/or quantitative survey research; mixed-methods experience is an advantage - Proficiency with qualitative analysis software and/or statistical software. - Strong writing skills, with the ability to produce polished reports and presentations for policy audiences. - Well-organised and able to manage fieldwork logistics independently; comfortable conducting fieldwork in residential estates, including some evenings and weekends. - Prior experience on policy-relevant research, and proficiency in Mandarin, Malay, or Tamil to support fieldwork (is preferred). Apply Now Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Copyright © 2025 Singapore Institute of Technology. All Rights Reserved. × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data centre \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary) prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Functional Cookies These cookies provide a better customer experience on this site, such as by remembering your login details, optimising video performance or providing us with information about how our site is used. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionalities that these third-parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Functional Cookies Show More Details Functional Cookies Provider Description Enabled Vimeo Vimeo is a video hosting, sharing and services platform focused on the delivery of video. Opting out of Vimeo cookies will disable your ability to watch or interact with Vimeo videos. Cookie Policy Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider Vimeo YouTube YouTube is a video-sharing service where users can create their own profile, upload videos, watch, like and comment on videos. Opting out of YouTube cookies will disable your ability to watch or interact with YouTube videos. Cookie Policy Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider YouTube Confirm My Choices Reject All Cookies Accept All Cookies"
